{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792776815,"nonce":"oQBBqFxqlJZ2Kzd2xfRSwTNN+rA=","title":"TypeScript: union, optional and computed-string module bindings","message":"Module bindings that functions use support numbers, booleans, static strings, class instances and arrays. Union and optional bindings report Unsupported: TypeScript keeps narrowing a module variable across calls, but the native union and optional reads select payloads from that narrowing, so a call that reassigns the binding would read the wrong payload. Supporting them needs narrowing that a call to a function writing the binding invalidates, at least for representation. String bindings assigned a computed string (concatenation, number formatting) also report Unsupported until the shared release analysis can prove a global keeps the string alive. Function-valued module bindings (const f = ...) report Unsupported as well. Official corpus rows stopping here include expressions/typeGuards/typeGuardsInClassMethods.ts and typeGuardsInFunction.ts (var var1: string | number) and expressions/nullishCoalescingOperator/nullishCoalescingOperator7.ts (var a: string | undefined). Parent: 751af73.","files":null}]}